    Microsoft Azure transcription service refactoring (#5876)
    
    The previous implementation of the Azure transcription service uses the
    Microsoft Speech SDK. On Linux systems it needs gstreamer installed as
    it will be used to extract and push audio stream to Azure. It comes with
    some complications. First of all, a memory leak. But also other issues
    we've found in production. This was the reason to refactor the code
    using the Azure Speech Services endpoints directly.
    
    Microsoft Azure Speech Services provide multiple ways to transcribe
    audio for specific use cases. As we want to transcript an static, not
    live, audio stream, the best option is the [Batch
    API](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/ai-services/speech-service/batch-transcription).
    It works like transcription services from other vendors
    
    - Upload audio track
    - Poll transcription job status
    - Download the result on success
    - Start an attach-transcript workflow
    
    To test the module you need an Microsoft account with at least a
    standard tear subscription. Free subscription does not work as
    documented
    [here](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/ai-services/speech-service/batch-transcription-create#prerequisites).
    Initially the code was written for Opencast 14. Since then we run it for
    two semesters in production. For the contribution it was rebased to
    develop and added the missing documentation. Some minor changes to
    handling captions (see
    [documentation](https://docs.opencast.org/develop/admin/#configuration/subtitles/))
    are also applied.
    
    
